      "title": "Identifying Conservative Vector Fields"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "73c25ee9-fef8-575b-9cb3-5456dd871c7b",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on $\\mathbb R^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $F=(4 x e^{2 x y})\\,i+(y e^{2 x y})\\,j$\n- **II.** $G=(x y + y^{2})\\,i+(x^{2} + x y)\\,j$\n- **III.** $H=(- \\cos{\\left(x + y \\right)})\\,i+(- \\cos{\\left(x + y \\right)})\\,j$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D=\\mathbb R^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D=\\mathbb R^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields.\n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P=4 x e^{2 x y}$ and $Q=y e^{2 x y}$. Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=8 x^{2} e^{2 x y},\\qquad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=2 y^{2} e^{2 x y}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal everywhere, we conclude that $F$ is not conservative on $\\mathbb R^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P=x y + y^{2}$ and $Q=x^{2} + x y$. Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=x + 2 y,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=2 x + y.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal everywhere, we conclude that $G$ is not conservative on $\\mathbb R^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P=- \\cos{\\left(x + y \\right)}$ and $Q=- \\cos{\\left(x + y \\right)}$. Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=\\sin{\\left(x + y \\right)},\\qquad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=\\sin{\\left(x + y \\right)}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on $\\mathbb R^{2},$ we conclude that $H$ is conservative on $\\mathbb R^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "17d734af-6c7c-5d35-a4fd-038c9f3393e1",
